In Angular, change detection is the process of detecting changes in the application's state and updating the view accordingly. By default, Angular triggers change detection for the entire component tree whenever any event occurs, which can be expensive and cause performance issues, especially for large and complex applications.

To update an Angular app to not do change detection on everything, but only on things you specify, you can use the `ChangeDetectionStrategy` to control the change detection behavior for each component.

Here are the steps to update your Angular app to use the `ChangeDetectionStrategy`:

1. Import `ChangeDetectionStrategy` from `@angular/core`.
```typescript
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy } from '@angular/core';
```
2. Set the `changeDetection` property of the component to `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush`.
```typescript
@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  templateUrl: './example.component.html',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  // component logic
}
```
This tells Angular to only run change detection for this component when its inputs change or when an event is triggered from this component.
3. Use the `ChangeDetectorRef` service to manually trigger change detection for the component.
```typescript
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy, ChangeDetectorRef } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  templateUrl: './example.component.html',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  constructor(private cd: ChangeDetectorRef) {}

  updateData() {
    // update component data
    this.cd.markForCheck();
  }
}
```
In this example, the `updateData()` method is called to update the component data. The `markForCheck()` method is called to notify Angular that the component needs to be checked for changes.

By using `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` and manually triggering change detection only when necessary, you can optimize the performance of your Angular app and reduce unnecessary change detection cycles.

