To configure unattended upgrades to only apply security updates, you will need to edit the `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50unattended-upgrades` file.

In this file, you should add the following line to only allow upgrades from the `Debian security` repository:
```
Unattended-Upgrade::Origins-Pattern {
        "origin=Debian,archive=stable,label=Debian-Security";
};
```
You also need to make sure that the `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/50unattended-upgrades` file has the following line so that only security updates are installed:
```
Unattended-Upgrade::Package-Blacklist {
    //
};
```
Once you have made these changes, save the file and then run the command:
```
sudo unattended-upgrade -d
```
This command will check for updates, and install only security updates if any are available.

You can also schedule the security upgrades to occur automatically by editing the `/etc/apt/apt.conf.d/10periodic` file, and setting the appropriate schedule, for example:
```
APT::Periodic::Unattended-Upgrade "7";
```
This will run unattended-upgrade every 7 days.

Please note that you should always backup your system before performing upgrades, especially if you are going to do it unattended.

