Asynchronous programming in C# allows for non-blocking operations to be executed, which can improve the responsiveness and performance of applications that perform I/O-bound or compute-bound tasks.

Here are the basic steps to create an asynchronous operation in C#:

1. Define an asynchronous method using the async modifier. This method should return a Task, Task<T>, or void.
2. Within the method, use the await keyword to indicate points at which the method can yield control to the calling thread while it waits for an asynchronous operation to complete.
3. Use the Task.Run method to create a new task that runs the long-running operation on a separate thread.
4. Optionally, you can use cancellation tokens to cancel the operation if it takes too long or is no longer needed.

Here is an example of an asynchronous method that downloads data from a web service:
```csharp
async Task<string> DownloadDataAsync(string url)
{
    using (var client = new WebClient())
    {
        var data = await client.DownloadStringTaskAsync(url);
        return data;
    }
}
```
In this example, the method is marked as async and returns a Task<string> object. Within the method, the WebClient.DownloadStringTaskAsync method is called asynchronously using the await keyword. This method downloads the data from the specified URL asynchronously and returns the result as a string.

To call this method from another method, you can use the await keyword as well:
```csharp
var data = await DownloadDataAsync("https://example.com");
```
When this line of code is executed, the calling thread will yield control to the system until the DownloadDataAsync method completes. Once the method is complete, the result will be returned to the calling thread.

Overall, asynchronous programming in C# can help improve the performance and responsiveness of applications by allowing them to execute non-blocking operations.

To pass a nullable CancellationToken to the Task.Run method as a parameter, you can use the null-coalescing operator to provide a default value in case the CancellationToken is null. Here's an example:
```scss
CancellationToken? cancellationToken = GetCancellationToken();

Task.Run(async () =>
{
    // Perform asynchronous operation
}, cancellationToken ?? CancellationToken.None);
```
In this example, the `GetCancellationToken()` method returns a nullable CancellationToken. The null-coalescing operator (`??`) is used to provide a default value of `CancellationToken.None` in case the CancellationToken is null.

By providing `CancellationToken.None` as the default value, the Task.Run method will create a task that is not cancellable if a null CancellationToken is passed in. If a non-null CancellationToken is passed in, the task will be created with the specified cancellation token and will be able to be cancelled if requested.

Note that if you're using C# 8 or later, you can also use the null-conditional operator (`?.`) to simplify the code slightly:
```scss
CancellationToken? cancellationToken = GetCancellationToken();

Task.Run(async () =>
{
    // Perform asynchronous operation
}, cancellationToken ??= CancellationToken.None);
```
In this example, the null-conditional operator is used to assign a default value of `CancellationToken.None` to the `cancellationToken` variable if it is null. This has the same effect as using the null-coalescing operator.

