The traveling salesman problem (TSP) is an NP-hard problem that involves finding the shortest possible route that visits a given set of locations and returns to the starting point.

Here's an example of how you could use the `Location` package to solve the TSP problem:
```
require __DIR__ . '/vendor/autoload.php';
use Location\Coordinate;
use Location\Distance\Vincenty;

$points = [
    new Coordinate(19.820664, -155.468066), // Mauna Kea Summit
    new Coordinate(20.709722, -156.253333), // Haleakala Summit
    new Coordinate(21.306944, -157.858333), // Diamond Head
    new Coordinate(21.277475, -157.822335), // Waikiki Beach
    new Coordinate(21.438912, -158.000057)  // Pearl Harbor
];

$calculator = new Vincenty();

$shortestDistance = INF;
$shortestRoute = [];

// Generate all possible routes
$routes = permutations($points);
foreach ($routes as $route) {
    // Calculate the distance of the current route
    $distance = 0;
    for ($i = 0; $i < count($route) - 1; $i++) {
        $distance += $calculator->getDistance($route[$i], $route[$i + 1]);
    }
    // Check if the current route is shorter than the previous shortest route
    if ($distance < $shortestDistance) {
        $shortestDistance = $distance;
        $shortestRoute = $route;
    }
}

echo "Shortest distance: " . $shortestDistance . " meters\n";
echo "Shortest route: " . implode(" -> ", $shortestRoute) . "\n";
```
This code uses an array of `Coordinate` objects to represent the locations to visit. The `permutations()` function is used to generate all possible routes.

Then, for each route, it calculates the distance using the `Vincenty` class and the `getDistance()` method, and keeps track of the shortest route found so far.

Finally, it outputs the shortest distance and the shortest route.

