```kotlin
data class Person(val name: String, val age: Int)

fun main() {
    val person = Person("John Doe", 30)

    // Obtain the class reference for the Person class
    val personClass = Person::class

    // Get the list of all properties declared in the class
    val properties = personClass.memberProperties

    // Iterate over the properties and print their names and values
    properties.forEach { property ->
        println("${property.name}: ${property.get(person)}")
    }
}
```
In this example, we create a `Person` data class with two properties: `name` and `age`. Then, we use reflection to obtain the class reference for the `Person` class, and to retrieve the list of all properties declared in the class. Finally, we iterate over the properties and print their names and values.

This is just a simple example, but reflection can be used for a wide range of more complex tasks, such as dynamically instantiating objects, accessing private members of a class, and modifying the behavior of existing classes, among others.

