    model.addAttribute("book", book);
    return "book-details";
}
```
In this example, the method retrieves the Book object with ID `id` from the Model and adds it as a model attribute with the name "book". The View "book-details" then reads this attribute and displays the book details.

In a POST request, the user typically submits a form with data that the web application uses to modify the Model. The Controller maps the submitted form data to a specific method that modifies the Model and then returns a View that displays the updated data.

For example, suppose the user submits a form to update the title of a book with ID 123. The Controller would have a method like the following:
```java
@PostMapping("/books/{id}")
public String updateBookTitle(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestParam("title") String newTitle, Model model) {
    Book book = bookService.findById(id);
    book.setTitle(newTitle);
    bookService.save(book);
    model.addAttribute("book", book);
    return "book-details";
}
```
In this example, the method retrieves the Book object with ID `id` from the Model, updates its title with the new value from the form, saves the updated Book to the Model, and then adds it as a model attribute with the name "book". The View "book-details" then reads this attribute and displays the updated book details.

Overall, the Model-View-Controller architecture separates the concerns of data (Model), user interface (View), and application logic (Controller) to create a more maintainable and flexible web application. In both GET and POST requests, the Controller maps the request to a specific method that updates the Model and returns a View that displays the Model data.

