To run multiple queries at once using GraphQL and Apollo Client, you can use Apollo's `useQuery` hook for each query, and then use the `useParallel` hook to run all the queries in parallel.

Here's an example of how to do this:
```
import { useParallel, useQuery } from '@apollo/client';

function MyComponent() {
  const query1 = useQuery(QUERY_1);
  const query2 = useQuery(QUERY_2);
  const query3 = useQuery(QUERY_3);

  const { data, errors, loading } = useParallel(query1, query2, query3);

  if (loading) return 'Loading...';
  if (errors) return 'An error occurred.';

  return (
    <div>
      {data.query1.result}
      {data.query2.result}
      {data.query3.result}
    </div>
  );
}
```
In this example, `QUERY_1`, `QUERY_2`, and `QUERY_3` are the GraphQL queries that you want to run in parallel. The `useParallel` hook returns an object with the `data`, `errors`, and `loading` for all of the queries. You can then use this data to render the results of the queries in your component.

Here's an example implementation of the `useParallel` hook:
```
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function useParallel(...queries) {
  const [data, setData] = useState({});
  const [errors, setErrors] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    Promise.all(queries.map(query => query.result)).then(results => {
      setData(results.reduce((prev, result) => ({ ...prev, ...result }), {}));
      setLoading(false);
    }).catch(error => {
      setErrors(error);
      setLoading(false);
    });
  }, [queries]);

  return { data, errors, loading };
}
```
This hook takes any number of queries as arguments, and then uses the `useState` hook to track the `data`, `errors`, and `loading` state for all of the queries. The `useEffect` hook is used to run all of the queries in parallel using the `Promise.all` method, and then update the state with the results.

To use this hook, you would call it like this:
```
const { data, errors, loading } = useParallel(query1, query2, query3);
```
Where `query1`, `query2`, and `query3` are the queries that you want to run in parallel. The hook will return an object with the combined `data`, `errors`, and `loading` state for all of the queries.

