I. Introduction
As an Android security researcher, one of the challenges is to create an application that runs persistently in the background, even if the parent application is no longer running or has been uninstalled. In this blog post, we will go through the steps required to build an Android application that spawns a background process named "shifuproc" which runs indefinitely and writes the current time to a file.

II. Creating the Android Application

1. Open Android Studio and create a new project.
2. Name the project "ShifuApp".
3. Choose "Empty Activity" as the project template.
4. Choose a package name for the application.
5. Click "Finish".

III. Implementing the "shifuproc" Process

1. Create a new Java class named "ShifuService".
2. Extend the class from the "Service" class.
3. Override the "onCreate" method and implement the following code:
```typescript
public class ShifuService extends Service {
    @Override
    public void onCreate() {
        super.onCreate();
        startShifuProc();
    }

    private void startShifuProc() {
        try {
            Runtime.getRuntime().exec("sh -c \"while true; do date >> /data/data/com.example.shifuapp/files/shifutime.txt; sleep 1; done\"");
        } catch (IOException e) {
            e.printStackTrace();
        }
    }
}
```
4. The code above starts the "shifuproc" process by using the "exec" method of the "Runtime" class to execute a shell command that writes the current time to a file located at "/data/data/com.example.shifuapp/files/shifutime.txt" every second.
5. In order to start the "shifuproc" process, we need to call the "startService" method from the main activity of the application. Add the following code to the "onCreate" method of the main activity:
```java
public class MainActivity extends AppCompatActivity {
    @Override
    protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

        startService(new Intent(this, ShifuService.class));
    }
}
```
IV. Ensuring Persistence of "shifuproc"

1. In order to ensure that "shifuproc" runs persistently, we need to start the service on boot. Add the following permission to the AndroidManifest.xml file:
```php
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED" />
```
2. Create a new Java class named "ShifuBootReceiver".
3. Extend the class from the "BroadcastReceiver" class.
4. Override the "onReceive" method and implement the following code:
```java
public class ShifuBootReceiver extends BroadcastReceiver {
    @Override
    public void onReceive(Context context, Intent intent) {
        if (intent.getAction().equals(Intent.ACTION_BOOT_COMPLETED)) {
            Intent serviceIntent = new Intent(context, ShifuService.class);
            context.startService(serviceIntent);
        }
    }
}
```
5. Register the "ShifuBootReceiver" class in the AndroidManifest.xml file by adding the following code:
```php
<receiver android:name=".ShifuBootReceiver">
    <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.BOOT_COMPLETED" />
    </intent-filter>
</receiver>
```
