You can create a custom Web Component by using the HTML custom element API and define a class that extends the HTMLElement class. To nest one custom Web Component inside another, you would simply include the inner component as an element within the template of the outer component.

Here's a basic example of how to do this:
```kotlin
class OuterComponent extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();

    const shadowRoot = this.attachShadow({mode: 'open'});
    shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
      <style>
        /* styles for outer component */
      </style>
      <inner-component></inner-component>
    `;
  }
}

customElements.define('outer-component', OuterComponent);

class InnerComponent extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();

    const shadowRoot = this.attachShadow({mode: 'open'});
    shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
      <style>
        /* styles for inner component */
      </style>
      <!-- template for inner component -->
    `;
  }
}

customElements.define('inner-component', InnerComponent);
```
This way, you can create complex and reusable UI components that can be composed of multiple nested components.

You can pass the inner components as children of the outer component in the HTML file, and then dynamically render them in the outer component's template. You can access the children of an element using the `children` property, and you can use the `appendChild` method to dynamically add elements to the shadow root.

Here's an example:
```kotlin
class OuterComponent extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();

    const shadowRoot = this.attachShadow({mode: 'open'});
    shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
      <style>
        /* styles for outer component */
      </style>
    `;

    // Get the inner components from the children
    const innerComponents = this.children;

    // Add the inner components to the shadow root
    for (const innerComponent of innerComponents) {
      shadowRoot.appendChild(innerComponent);
    }
  }
}

customElements.define('outer-component', OuterComponent);

class InnerComponent extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();

    const shadowRoot = this.attachShadow({mode: 'open'});
    shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
      <style>
        /* styles for inner component */
      </style>
      <!-- template for inner component -->
    `;
  }
}

customElements.define('inner-component', InnerComponent);
```
And in your HTML file, you would use the components like this:
```php
<outer-component>
  <inner-component></inner-component>
  <inner-component></inner-component>
  <!-- more inner-components as needed -->
</outer-component>
```
This way, you can dynamically add as many `inner-component` elements as needed to the `outer-component`, and the outer component will render all of its children as inner components.

If you want the inner component to represent a list, you can define its template accordingly, and then pass an array of data to the outer component which it can use to dynamically generate instances of the inner component.

Here's an example:
```kotlin
class OuterComponent extends HTMLElement {
  constructor() {
    super();

    this.data = [];

    const shadowRoot = this.attachShadow({mode: 'open'});
    shadowRoot.innerHTML = `
