convert this typescript code to flutter
filesize(url: string, callback: any) {
 var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
 xhr.open("HEAD", url, true); // Notice "HEAD" instead of "GET",
 // to get only the header
 xhr.onreadystatechange = function () {
 if (this.readyState == this.DONE) {
 callback(parseInt(xhr.getResponseHeader("Content-Length")));
 }
 };
 xhr.send();
 }

Here is the equivalent code in Flutter:
```dart
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

Future<void> getFileSize(String url, Function(int) callback) async {
  final response = await http.head(Uri.parse(url));
  final contentLength = int.parse(response.headers['content-length'] ?? '0');
  callback(contentLength);
}
```
Note that instead of using `XMLHttpRequest`, we use the `http` package that provides an easy-to-use API for making HTTP requests. The `head` method is used to send a HEAD request to the server and retrieve only the headers. Once we get the response, we parse the `Content-Length` header to get the file size and pass it to the callback function.

Also, note that in the original code, the `callback` parameter is of type `any`, which means it can accept any type of function. In the Flutter code, we explicitly specify the type of the `callback` parameter as `Function(int)`, which means it should be a function that accepts an integer parameter.

