To find the longest common subsequence of two strings, we can use dynamic programming. The idea is to build up a solution by comparing the characters of the two strings one by one, starting from the beginning.

At each step, we have two options:

* If the characters at the current position in the two strings are the same, we can include this character in the common subsequence and move to the next position in both strings.
* If the characters are different, we have to choose which string to move forward in. In this case, we take the maximum of the two possibilities: either moving forward in the first string, or moving forward in the second string.

We can use a two-dimensional array to store the length of the longest common subsequence at each step. The first dimension represents the position in the first string, and the second dimension represents the position in the second string. Then, we can fill in this array using the above logic, starting from the first character in both strings and moving towards the last character. The length of the longest common subsequence will be the value stored in the last cell of the array.

Here is the pseudocode for the algorithm:
